export const GUIDE_FILES: Record<string, string> = {
  "llms.txt": "# Yardhand\n\n> A self-hosted gateway: one endpoint for every tool behind it, from MCP servers and REST APIs its owner connected.\n> Find a tool by what you want to do, read its input schema, run it. Over MCP at `/mcp`, or over REST here.\n> Send `Authorization: Bearer <key>` on every call (one key per client), or an OAuth token the owner approves.\n\n## Start\n\n- MCP clients: connect to `/mcp`. You get `search_tools`, `describe_tool`, `call_tool`, `get_result`,\n  `list_upstreams`, `read_resource`, `get_playbook`, and any tools pinned for your key. The server instructions list\n  the upstreams you may use, and mark those with a manual. Playbooks are also listed as prompts (slash commands).\n- REST clients: `GET /api` links to everything. The usual path is three calls:\n  1. `GET /api/tools?q=<what you want to do>`: tools across every upstream, best match first.\n  2. `GET /api/tools/{name}`: the tool's description and JSON Schema input.\n  3. `POST /api/calls` with `{\"tool\": \"<name>\", \"arguments\": {…}}`, or `POST /api/tools/{name}/calls` with the\n     arguments as the whole body. The answer carries the tool's own result (`result.content`, and\n     `result.structuredContent` when it declares an output schema).\n- Typed clients: `GET /api/tools/openapi.json` describes every tool your key may use as an OpenAPI 3.1 operation.\n- The owner manages the gateway in the browser at `/app` (connections, keys, reviews, the call log). Everything there\n  is an `/api` route too, but those routes answer only the owner's key: a client key gets 403.\n\n## Manuals, playbooks and links\n\nSome upstreams say more than their tools do:\n\n- A **manual**: how the upstream wants its tools used. Read it once before its tools: `read_resource`\n  `gateway://u/<upstream>/manual` (MCP), or `GET /api/manuals/{name}`. `GET /api/manuals` lists them; MCP clients with\n  the Skills extension also get them as skills (`skill://<manual>/SKILL.md`). The gateway adds a short note at the\n  top saying how its tools and links are named here; the rest is as the upstream wrote it.\n- **Playbooks**: the upstream's step-by-step way to do one task (its MCP prompts). `search_tools` (and\n  `GET /api/tools?q=…`, in `playbooks`) suggests one when it fits; `get_playbook` or\n  `GET /api/playbooks/{name}/messages?arguments={…}` fills it in. Named `<upstream>-<prompt>`.\n- **Links** a tool returns, like `cookbook://recipes/123`: read them as they came with `read_resource` or\n  `GET /api/resources?uri=…`; the gateway finds the upstream by the scheme. `GET /api/links` lists the kinds.\n\nReading any of these uses the upstream's credential and is logged like a call, but needs no write scope.\n\n## Pack keys\n\nA key can be locked to a **pack**: a kit for one kind of work, curated by the owner. `GET /api/me` says so\n(`pack`). A pack key sees only the pack: its tools are listed directly over MCP (in the pack's own wording), its\ndescription is the server instructions, and search, `describe_tool`, playbooks, manuals and calls stay inside it. A\ncall to anything else is refused (403) and logged; the per-upstream passthrough (`/u/{id}/mcp`) is refused too. A\npack key opens only the links its tools returned in the last day (through `/u/{upstream}/r/…` and `read_resource`).\nKeys can expire: a 403 says when.\n\n## Rules worth knowing\n\n- Tool names are `<upstream>.<tool>`, e.g. `cookbook.research_recipes`.\n- A tool that changes things (`needsWrite: true`, `[changes things]` in MCP) needs a key with write scope; a\n  read-only key never sees those tools.\n- The owner can switch tools off: one tool, a group of a connection's tools, or everything on a connection that\n  changes or deletes things. An off tool is left out of lists and searches; calling it anyway is a 403 whose `detail`\n  says which switch is off. Ask the owner rather than looking for another way round.\n- Over MCP, a result bigger than your key's cap is cut: you get page 1 and a call id; `get_result` (or\n  `GET /api/calls/{id}/result?page=2`) gives the rest for an hour. REST answers are never cut.\n- `POST` runs accept `Idempotency-Key`: a retried request with the same key answers the first result again.\n- Calls are rate-limited per key (`RateLimit` headers; 429 with `Retry-After` when over).\n- Errors are `application/problem+json` with a `detail` that says what to do next: arguments that don't match\n  the tool's input schema are a 400 listing each bad field in `errors`, before the upstream is called. A tool\n  that ran and reported its own failure answers 200 with `status: \"tool_error\"` and `result.isError: true`.\n- Links in a result that point at an upstream's own host are rewritten to `/u/{upstream}/r/<path>`: open them\n  with the same key (read-only, through the gateway).\n- A connection's tools come in groups (`groups` on `GET /api/upstreams`, printed by `list_upstreams`): narrow a\n  search to one with `group` and `upstream` (`search_tools`, or `GET /api/tools?upstream=…&group=…`).\n- In lists a tool has a short `summary`; `GET /api/tools/{name}` gives the full `description` and schemas.\n- Lists are HAL: follow `_links.next` until it is absent.\n- Send W3C trace context (a `traceparent` header on REST, `_meta.traceparent` on MCP) and each call is recorded as a\n  span inside your own trace; without it, each call is a trace of its own. Only metadata is traced, never arguments\n  or results.\n\n## Endpoints\n\n{{api.endpoints}}\n\n## MCP tools\n\n{{mcp.tools}}\n",
}
